--TEST--
`Core\Uri`'s port is `0-65535` at both ends, absent where none and where an empty one was written, and `with` draws the same bound from the other direction — spec § 12, RFC 3986 § 3.2.3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// RFC 3986 § 3.2.3's *grammar* is `*DIGIT` while its *prose* defines the
// component as a TCP port number, and `port()` is where the two are
// reconciled. So the bound is named at both of its ends rather than in the
// middle: the last value each side accepts, beside the first it refuses.
final class Show {
    public static function port(string $text): string {
        ?int $port = Core\Uri::parse($text)->port();
        if ($port == null) {
            return "null";
        }
        return $port as string;
    }
}

// Accepted, bottom end to top. `00080` is the grammar being `*DIGIT` rather
// than a canonical number — the digits are read, not the spelling. An empty
// port and an absent one are both `null`, which is § 3.2.3's own instruction
// to a producer and `port_of`'s one departure from giving back what was
// written; a reference with no authority at all has nowhere to write one.
array<string> $texts = [
    "http://h:0/",
    "http://h:1/",
    "http://h:65535/",
    "http://h:00080/",
    "http://h:/",
    "http://h/",
    "/p",
];
foreach ($texts as string $text) {
    echo "[", $text, "] port=", Show::port($text), "\n";
}

// One past the top, and two digit runs past it — including one no integer type
// holds at all, which is the same refusal rather than a second one. Storing it
// would answer with a number no caller could dial and truncating it would
// answer with a different URI's port.
array<string> $refused = [
    "http://h:65536/",
    "http://h:99999/",
    "http://h:99999999999999999999/",
];
foreach ($refused as string $refusedText) {
    try {
        string $shown = Show::port($refusedText);
        echo "[", $refusedText, "] port=", $shown, "\n";
    } catch (Throwable $high) {
        echo "[", $refusedText, "] refused\n";
    }
}

// `with`'s `port` option is declared `int`, so it reaches the bound from the
// other side and can be spelled *below* it as well — which is why both ends
// are one refusal here rather than the grammar catching the negative one a
// step earlier and reporting a stray byte.
array<int> $accepted = [0, 1, 8080, 65535];
foreach ($accepted as int $port) {
    echo "with ", $port, " -> ", Core\Uri::parse("http://h/")->with({port: $port})->toString(), "\n";
}
array<int> $outside = [-1, 65536];
foreach ($outside as int $beyond) {
    try {
        string $built = Core\Uri::parse("http://h/")->with({port: $beyond})->toString();
        echo "with ", $beyond, " -> ", $built, "\n";
    } catch (Throwable $moved) {
        echo "with ", $beyond, ": ", $moved->message, "\n";
    }
}

// The two members agree over the accepted range: what `with` writes is what
// `port` reads back, counted rather than read off any one line.
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($accepted as int $round) {
    ?int $back = Core\Uri::parse("http://h/")->with({port: $round})->port();
    if ($back == $round) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}
echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/", Core\Arr::count($accepted) as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
[http://h:0/] port=0
[http://h:1/] port=1
[http://h:65535/] port=65535
[http://h:00080/] port=80
[http://h:/] port=null
[http://h/] port=null
[/p] port=null
[http://h:65536/] refused
[http://h:99999/] refused
[http://h:99999999999999999999/] refused
with 0 -> http://h:0/
with 1 -> http://h:1/
with 8080 -> http://h:8080/
with 65535 -> http://h:65535/
with -1: Core\Uri::with(): the authority's port is not a TCP port number. RFC 3986 § 3.2.3 admits any run of digits and defines the component as a port, so a value outside 0-65535 has no `int` this member could answer with
with 65536: Core\Uri::with(): the authority's port is not a TCP port number. RFC 3986 § 3.2.3 admits any run of digits and defines the component as a port, so a value outside 0-65535 has no `int` this member could answer with
agreed=4/4
